# SRG-APP-000840-AU-000310  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must for password-based authentication, update the
# list of passwords when organizational passwords are suspected to have been
# compromised directly or indirectly.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000840-AU-000310
group:           V-263576
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   ORGANIZATIONAL
grade:           D
decidable:       manual
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-004060
control_rev5:    IA-5

contribution:    None toward the list: the product has no deny list to update on suspicion
                 of compromise. What the application can do once a specific credential is
                 suspected is force its replacement - an administrator resets the account
                 and the [splunk_auth] history and complexity settings prevent reuse of a
                 stored previous password - but that acts on one account, not on a list of
                 compromised passwords, and there is no setting that represents such an
                 event.

external:        Detection and declaration of suspected password compromise, and the
                 resulting update to the list - carried by the organization's incident
                 response and account-management procedures.
external:        Holding and updating the list - carried by the directory service or
                 identity provider where authType is LDAP, SAML, or ProxySSO.

manual_reason:   The requirement is to update a list the product does not hold, triggered
                 by an event no configuration value records; nothing observable in Splunk
                 configuration can decide it.

citation:        splunk-spec-files/authentication.conf.spec:437-600 ([splunk_auth];
                 enablePasswordHistory at :554 and passwordHistoryCount at :561 prevent
                 reuse per account, not list maintenance)
finding:         Not a finding against the application: Splunk Enterprise 10.4.2 holds no
                 password deny list and has no setting representing suspected compromise,
                 so this requirement is carried by the organization's incident response
                 procedure and by the identity provider that holds the list; this
                 determination is from the perspective of the application layer alone.
